      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "We evaluated the clinical value of a single measurement of high-sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs- CRP) in patients presenting to the emergency department with chest pain. We screened 408 consecutive patients of whom 292 comprised the final cohort for this study. Hs-CRP measured in the emergency department (ED) in patients presenting with chest pain and admitted for evaluation of acute myocardial infarction was neither sensitive nor specific in predicting acute myocardial infarction, myocardial ischemia on SPECT imaging, need for coronary revascularization, or cardiovascular or all-cause rehospitalization at 30 days. In addition, use of a specific CRP cut off >1 was not associated with an increase in all-cause rehospitalization at 30 days."},
